\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|    |    |KWF1|    |NWF0|RWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b7-b5, P: b7-a6, P: b7-b6, N: b8-a6, N: b8-c6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, P: d7-d5, P: d7-d6, P: e5-e4, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, R: f8-e8, R: f8-d8, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, K: g8-h8, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black and I'm in a very dangerous position. White has a Queen aggressively placed on a6, deep in my territory on the 6th rank. My King on g8 has castled kingside (with the Rook on f8), but White's Queen is creating serious threats. My pieces are mostly undeveloped on the back rank, and I have a pawn advanced to e5. White's king is on e1 and appears relatively safe.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most critical moves:\n\n1. **R: f8-e8** - Activates my rook along the e-file where White's king sits. This could create counterplay and pressure.\n\n2. **N: b8-c6** - Develops my knight to a central square, helping control the center and potentially supporting other pieces.\n\n3. **R: f8-d8** - Activates the rook along the d-file, which could be useful but less immediately threatening than the e-file.\n\n4. **P: d7-d5** - Opens up the position and could help develop my dark-squared bishop, but might be too slow given the immediate threats.\n\n5. **K: g8-h8** - Moves my king to relative safety in the corner, but this seems passive and doesn't address White's threats.\n\nThe White Queen on a6 is very threatening, but I need to create active counterplay rather than just defend passively. Moving my rook to e8 puts immediate pressure on White's exposed king on e1, which could force White to deal with defensive concerns rather than continue attacking.\n\nReason: R: f8-e8 is the best move because it activates my most powerful piece (the rook) and creates immediate tactical pressure against White's king on the e-file. This forces White to consider defensive measures and gives me the best chance to create counterplay in a difficult position. Development and activity are crucial when under pressure.\n\nAnswer: R: f8-e8"}
