knight on a powerful central outpost where it cannot be easily challenged by White pawns (the d4 square is well-supported and White has no pawn on c3 to challenge it effectively - wait, there IS a pawn on c3, but it's already there). The knight on d4 will control key squares (b3, c2, e2, f3, b5, c6, e6, f5), put pressure on White's position, and is very difficult to dislodge. This is much more forcing and active than simply capturing with the pawn.\n\nAnswer: N: c6-d4"}
