\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, P: c5-c4, R: d6-c6, R: d6-b6, R: d6-a6, R: d6-d5, R: d6-d4, R: d6-d3, R: d6-d2, R: d6-d1, R: d6-d7, R: d6-d8, R: d6-e6, R: d6-f6, R: d6-g6, P: g5-g4, K: g8-f7, K: g8-f8, K: g8-g7, K: g8-h7, K: g8-h8, P: h6-h5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black in an endgame position. The material count shows I have a King on g8, a Rook on d6, and 5 pawns (a7, b7, c5, g5, h6). White has only a King on f5 and one pawn on a3. I have a significant material advantage with my rook and extra pawns, so I should be winning this position.\n\nThe White King is actively placed on f5, relatively close to my kingside pawns. My rook is centralized on d6, which is strong. My goal should be to use my rook to support my pawn advances while keeping the White King at bay, ultimately promoting a pawn or delivering checkmate.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider the most promising moves:\n\n1. **R: d6-d5+** - This gives check, forcing the White King to move. This is tactically strong as it disrupts White's plans and gains tempo.\n\n2. **R: d6-d1** - Moves the rook to the back rank, which could be useful for supporting pawn pushes from behind, but less immediately forcing.\n\n3. **P: c5-c4** - Advances a passed pawn, but the White King on f5 could potentially stop it.\n\n4. **P: g5-g4** - Advances the g-pawn, but this might weaken my king's position and the White King is well-placed to stop it.\n\n5. **P: a7-a5 or b7-b5** - Creating passed pawns on the queenside away from White's King.\n\nReason: **R: d6-d5+** is the strongest move. It gives check to the White King on f5, forcing it to move and losing tempo. After the King moves, I can use my rook actively to support my passed pawns while keeping White's King restricted. This forcing move maintains my winning advantage and makes it harder for White to coordinate defense. In endgames with a significant material advantage, active piece play and forcing moves help convert the advantage most efficiently.\n\nAnswer: R: d6-d5"}
